course: dictionary
lesson: 15427
title: Standardisation
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Standardisation is a long word, but you can break it into pieces. Standard means a rule or measure that everyone agrees to use. The ending -isation means the process of doing something, so standardisation is the process of making things follow one agreed way.
explain: «The factory used standardisation to make sure every plug fitted every socket.» The clues are plug and socket: the plugs had to be made the same, so they would fit together. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word.
explain: «Standardisation of spelling, as in Johnson's dictionary in 1755, helped people agree on how to write words.» The clues are spelling and dictionary: everyone agreed on one way to write each word. Always check that your guess makes sense in the sentence.
fact: Standardisation means making things the same, or agreeing on one standard way of doing something.
fact: The ending -isation shows a process, so «standardisation» is the process of making things follow a common standard.
fact: Standard units, such as metric measures, let everyone compare their answers in maths and science.
fact: Standard plugs, USB-C chargers and A4 paper are everyday examples of standardisation in technology.
fact: Railway time and time zones are examples of standardisation of time, so trains and clocks can work together.
exercise: «The shop used standardisation so that every tin of beans held the same weight.» What does standardisation mean here? | Painting everything a different colour | Losing track of time | *Making things the same, following one agreed way | Making things bigger than before
exercise: Why are USB-C chargers an example of standardisation? | *They all fit the same type of port, so they work together | They are the cheapest chargers in the shop | They come in lots of different colours | They only charge at night
exercise: The ending -isation in standardisation tells you what? | It means the word is a small object | It shows the word is a question | It shows the word is a person's name | *It shows a process or action of making something
test: «The school agreed on one standard ruler length so every class measured the same way.» What is the main idea? | Rulers were banned from the school | Every class has a different ruler | *Everyone agreed to use the same measure | The school bought more pencils
test: Why did Johnson's dictionary help standardisation of spelling? | *It gave people one agreed spelling to follow | It was written only for children | It made spelling harder for everyone | It removed all the words from English
exam: Which is the best meaning of standardisation? | Something that only happens in science | A type of food | Making things as different as possible | *Making things the same, or agreeing on one standard way of working
